# PROLOGUE — "Soundcheck", part 2: the alley behind the Pink Wyvern.
# Objectives come from src/content/objectives.ts; repeat examines branch on seen.<scene id>.

@scene prologue.the_letter
# PLOT: Establish the Cyrano letters, Rex's secret (he can't read), and his "only I bully Adder" code.
# CHARACTER: Rex's ego and his buried decency; Eddar's talent and dry wit; the barrel as a running gag.
@bgm alley_rex
@cam focus rex
NARRATOR: The alley smells like fryer oil and sea salt. Rex Valor is leaning on the dumpster like it owes him rent.
REX [smug]: Adder. My man. My scribe. My little pencil.
EDDAR: Rex. You're in the alley. Voluntarily.
REX: Rex goes where Rex is needed. And tonight Rex needs a letter.
GRUBB: Rex needs a letter!
REX: Thank you, Grubb.
REX [smug]: Princess Lyra. Solara. Sky city. Hair like a sunset on a Tuesday. She's going to be at the Hero's Gala, and when the Rubric says my name tonight, she's going to have a letter from me in her hand.
EDDAR: You don't know they're saying your name.
REX: Adder. Look at me.
NARRATOR: He flexes. One pauldron catches the neon and throws a little gold light across the bricks.
REX: Who else would they say?
LEDGER: Entry #2,208: He has a point. It is the only point he has ever had, and he wields it like a lance. I hate it with my whole and considerable heart.
REX: Okay. Pencil out. "Dear Princess. You're hot."
EDDAR: Inspiring. Can I make one tiny suggestion?
REX: Is it "but in poetry"? Because Rex was getting there.
? Write it beautifully. -> beautiful {harmony:+1}
? Write it beautifully, with one tiny joke at Rex's expense. -> joke {bond_rex:-1}
? Write exactly what he said. -> literal {bond_rex:-2}
* literal
EDDAR: "Dear Princess. You're hot." Period. Signed, Rex.
REX: Read it back.
EDDAR: "Dear Princess. You're hot."
REX: ...
REX [hurt]: Adder. I pay you three tokens a letter because I'm a GENEROUS LEGEND, not because I want to sound like Grubb.
GRUBB: Hey.
REX: Do it right.
@goto beautiful
* joke
@flag p.joke
EDDAR: Writing. "...and though I am but a humble jouster, with hair no mortal comb has ever tamed..."
REX [smile]: Ha! That's good. That's true. Keep that.
@goto beautiful
* beautiful
NARRATOR: Eddar writes. It comes out the way it always does when he isn't writing for himself: easy, and true, and a little sad.
EDDAR: "Princess. They tell me the sky over Solara never really goes dark, that the city holds the last of the sunset in its towers."
EDDAR: "I've never seen it. But I think I know what that's like. Some people walk into a room and it stops being night."
GRUBB: ...Whoa.
REX [hurt]: ...
REX: Yeah. Yeah, that's what Rex meant.
EDDAR: Want me to read the rest?
REX: No. No, Rex's eyes are tired. Rex trusts you. Seal it.
LEDGER: Entry #2,209: Fourteen letters to a princess I have never seen, each signed by a man who has never read them. Somewhere in a sky city, a girl is falling in love with a ghost. I hope she's happy. I hope the ghost is, too.
@sfx coins
REX: Three tokens. Plus one, because Rex is in a giving mood and destiny is in the air.
@set tokens += 4
@set bond_rex += 1
EDDAR: Thanks, Rex.
REX [smug]: Don't thank me yet.
@sfx barrel
@cam shake
NARRATOR: Rex picks him up by the jacket, one-handed, and drops him into the trash barrel like he's posting a letter.
EDDAR: ...There it is.
REX: Tradition, Adder. Can't break tradition on Proclamation Night. Bad luck.
VINNIE: HA! Look at the mop kid! In the BIN! Where he LIVES!
@cam focus rex
NARRATOR: A dock-worker named Vinnie is in the alley mouth, pointing and laughing. Rex turns very slowly.
REX: Hey.
REX: Hey.
REX [angry]: Nobody pushes Adder around but me.
@sfx punch
@cam shake
NARRATOR: Vinnie is suddenly lying down, in a different part of the alley, rethinking several life choices.
REX [smug]: You're welcome, dweeb.
GRUBB: You're welcome, dweeb!
REX: Grubb.
GRUBB: Sorry.
? "...Thanks, Rex." -> thanks {bond_rex:+1}
? "You know that's not how protecting someone works, right?" -> works {harmony:+1}
* thanks
LEDGER: Entry #2,210: Thanked my assailant for his gallantry toward me against a lesser assailant, from inside the receptacle into which he had assailed me. Let the record show I was raised with manners, if not a spine.
@goto exit
* works
REX: Course it is. You're still in one piece, aren't you? That's protecting.
REX: Mostly one piece. One and a bit.
@goto exit
* exit
REX: C'mon, Grubb. Big night. The Rubric's gonna say my name and Rex wants to be holding a drink when they do.
@cmd exit_npc rex
@cmd exit_npc grubb
@flag p.letter_done
@end

@scene prologue.vinnie
# CHARACTER: a bully's bully, now horizontal. Eddar is kind to him anyway.
@if p.letter_done
@if seen.prologue.vinnie>=2
VINNIE: Zzz. ...Five more minutes, Ma.
EDDAR: Sure thing, Vinnie. I'll tell the dumpster you're resting.
@end
@endif
@if seen.prologue.vinnie>=1
VINNIE: Hey. Mop kid. Was that Rex Valor who hit me? THE Rex Valor?
EDDAR: The very same.
VINNIE: ...Nice. Gonna tell my kids about this. If I can remember having kids.
@end
@endif
VINNIE: Ugh. Is the sky supposed to be spinning?
EDDAR: Only on Proclamation Night. Stay down, Vinnie.
@end
@endif
VINNIE: What're you lookin' at, mop kid?
@end

@scene prologue.dumpster
# PLOT: End-of-build hook: Sable's first glimpse. CHARACTER: she looks at him, not at the Hero.
@if !p.letter_done
EDDAR: The dumpster. I should probably deal with Rex first. Or rather, Rex will probably deal with me.
@end
@endif
@if p.sable_seen
EDDAR: Trash is out. Dignity is negotiable.
@end
@endif
@sfx trash
EDDAR: Trash: out. Dignity: also out, but in a separate bag.
@sfx engine
@cam focus street
NARRATOR: Something goes past the mouth of the alley, low and fast and almost silent. A black light-cycle, trailing violet light.
NARRATOR: It stops. The rider is a woman in black wraps and a black visor, silver hair cropped short. The visor turns, past the Wyvern, past the crowds heading to the Cathedral.
NARRATOR: It stops on Eddar.
EDDAR: ...Hi?
NARRATOR: She looks for one second longer than a stranger should. Then the light-cycle is gone, and the alley is just an alley again.
LEDGER: Entry #2,211: Tonight a stranger on a black machine looked at me as though I were a word she had been trying to remember. One second, no more. I have been looked at before, chiefly by Rex, and chiefly before impact. This was different. Logging it in case it never happens again.
@flag p.sable_seen
@bgm alley_night
@card END OF BUILD 0.1 — PROCLAMATION NIGHT CONTINUES IN THE NEXT BUILD
@end

@scene prologue.barrel
# CHARACTER: running gag; Eddar's grace under humiliation.
@if p.letter_done
@if seen.prologue.barrel>=2
EDDAR: Some men have a family crest. I have a barrel. Same thing, structurally.
@end
@endif
EDDAR: My barrel. We've been through a lot together. More than I've been through with most people.
@end
@endif
EDDAR: A trash barrel. Clean, as far as trash barrels go. Rex has standards.
@end

@scene prologue.cat
# CHARACTER: Eddar's gentleness. Encore will matter later (she always comes back).
@if seen.prologue.cat>=1
EDDAR: Encore blinks at me, slowly. In cat, that's either "I love you" or "you are furniture." I'll take either.
@end
@endif
EDDAR: The alley cat. Mama Dee calls her Encore, because she always comes back.
EDDAR: She judges me less than everyone inside. I bring her fish.
@end

@scene prologue.exit_sign
# CHARACTER: the quiet ache under the jokes.
@if seen.prologue.exit_sign>=1
EDDAR: It flickers on the X. EIT. Even the way out isn't sure of itself tonight.
@end
@endif
EDDAR: A neon EXIT sign, buzzing pink. Every night it tells me there's a way out. Every night I go back inside.
@end

@scene prologue.alley_mouth
# PLOT: soft wall at the end of the build.
@if !p.sable_seen
EDDAR: Out there it's all Proclamation crowds and Rubric banners. I should finish the trash first. Mama Dee would find me. She always finds me.
@end
@endif
EDDAR: The Cathedral bells are starting. The next part of this night isn't built yet. (End of build 0.1.)
@end
